--TEST--
The two constructs the front end accepts and nothing below it compiles yet are refused at their own site — `require` used for its value is `E0704` (ADR 0021 § 3, `mwl-ir` gap 22) and `spawn script` is `E0703` (ADR 0006, M5) — while `require` as a statement is whole and reports nothing
--FILE--
<?mwl
// ADR 0021 § 3 gives `require`'s value the `mixed` it cannot know
// statically. What is missing is below the checker: `mwl_hir` merges the
// target's declarations at compile time and the site lowers to nothing,
// so the target's own top-level statements never run and there is no
// `return` for the value to come from — `mwl-ir`'s known gap 22.
mixed $c = require "cfg.mwl";

// The statement form is whole and is what the refusal points at: the
// class the target declares is reachable from here with no value ever
// changing hands, and no diagnostic below names either of these lines.
require "cfg.mwl";
echo Cfg::name();

// ADR 0006's isolate spawn, which nothing below the checker compiles yet
// (`docs/plan/m5.md`). Refusing it where it is written is the only
// reading that cannot silently do nothing.
spawn script "cfg.mwl";
--FILE cfg.mwl--
<?mwl
class Cfg {
    public static function name(): string {
        return "cfg";
    }
}
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0704]: `require`'s value is not available
  --> %s:7:12
  |
7 | mixed $c = require "cfg.mwl";
  |            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ this `require` is used for what it hands back
  = help: write `require '…';` as a statement — the target's declarations are already merged at compile time. ADR 0021 § 3's `mixed` value needs the target's own top-level statements to run, which `mwl-ir`'s known gap 22 is about

error[E0703]: `spawn script` is not compiled yet
   --> %s:18:1
   |
18 | spawn script "cfg.mwl";
   | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ no isolate is created here
   = help: ADR 0006's isolates arrive with `docs/plan/m5.md`, together with the value-crossing copy a spawn boundary needs. There is no same-frame spelling of this to fall back on

error: aborting due to 2 errors
